A||N||5||FieroSTETZ||oil on the passenger side floor- heater core? help!||||4||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||10-06-2001||11:48 AM||||i think it might be the heater core, laid out a piece of paper and its getting oily dripping on it, in about 5 minutes the whole paper was saturated. sounds like the heater core? is there any way i can disconnect it? I have a 9 hour drive after work tonight and no time to make a repair. havent needed the heater in 2 years, so shouldnt need it for the next few days. any ideas?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.shawnrocks.com/Animation1.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||10-06-2001||11:57 AM||||It should be coolant coming out on the passenger side. The quick fix is to open up the hood, take off the windshield resevoir, (I think you can do it with it on but it gives you some more space.) and pull the rubber hoses that you see there to plug them with appropriate sized bolts. Or if you really have the resources fashion a pipe to connect them both. This would by pass the heater core and get you on the way without losing coolant.<P>     I have had a core go bad and I used the bolt method to get home.<P>Oh, and one other possiblility. And it sounds really weird but damned if it didn't work. <P>Pepper. <P>I was on a ride with my club up to Julian and I blew the heater core. Leaking pretty badly, steady stream on the ground. We had just stopped for lunch and I told Keith Huff and John Parenti about it. I was seriously bummed. John handed me one of the pepper shakers and told me to put it in. I thought he was nuts but had little else to lose. It got me through the rest of the trip and all the way back home! Would have NEVER believed it unless I had seen it myself.<P>Since you are not in such dire straits, I would go with the bypass to be sure.<P>Good luck.||||reg||6
Z||000002||WampusCat||10-06-2001||12:01 PM||||Does your car have A/C?  Can't think of why oil?!?! would be leaking from heater core.  Could be from the air conditioning system.  I just changed a heater core a couple of days ago.  Not too hard, just have to remove the window washer tank to get to the hoses. Also had to remove the subwoofer box to get the core out. It's kinda hard to bypass the heater core with a piece of tubing because one hose is 3/4" and the other is 5/8" (approx.) ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-06-2001||12:10 PM||||coolant can have an oily feel to it.<P>Don't bother wasting time with sealants on that. If you have to drive it then bypass the core using one of the above methods until you can get a new core or get this one repaired. I usually just get the new one, repairing it doesn't save much if any thing and it's liable to start leaking someplace else in the near future.<P>If you have a factory sunroof, remove the storage rack for easier acces to the hoses along with the washer tank if need be.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||10-06-2001||12:11 PM|||| What WDKFIRO said! Here is a photo. It.s better to form a loop with a section of pipe. <BR>If you can scrounge a hacksaw blade you may be able to cut enough off of the core to connect. Make sure you top the cooling system afterward of course.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/heat.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||SLYGUY||10-06-2001||09:15 PM||||I've always been told that when your heater core goes out and you want to bypass it be sure to connect the hoses together. Your cooling system was designed to flow and not be stopped by anything. <P>If you plug it be careful and watch your temperature.<P>Sly||||reg||1
